# Every file here is stored as LF and checked out as LF, on every platform.
#
# Git already stores LF; what this pins down is the checkout. On Windows the
# global `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites text files to CRLF on the way out, and a
# fresh checkout is exactly what a `git` install is — so the installed copy came
# down as CRLF while the npm tarball, packed from a working tree, kept whatever
# the tree happened to hold. The two install paths were handing over different
# bytes for the same release. An attribute is more specific than the config, so
# this wins and both paths agree.
#
# The update manifest is unaffected either way: `tools/make-update-manifest.py`
# digests LF-normalised bytes, so these attributes fix the difference instead of
# hiding it — and `_fault_inject_update.py` injection A exists to keep the
# normalisation honest for copies that were installed before this file existed.
#
# `client.js` is the file this was noticed on: it is generated by esbuild, which
# always emits LF, so it was the one shipped file that a CRLF checkout changed.
* text=auto eol=lf
